# Step 1 — Identify YugabyteDB Log Location

On each YugabyteDB node:

```bash
ps -ef | grep yb-tserver
```

You will see something like:

```text
--log_dir=/home/yugabyte/yb-data/tserver/logs
```

Example:

```text
/home/yugabyte/yb-data/tserver/logs/
```

Files:

```text
yb-tserver.INFO
yb-tserver.WARNING
yb-tserver.ERROR
```

Master logs:

```text
/home/yugabyte/yb-data/master/logs/
```

Example:

```text
yb-master.INFO
```

---

# Step 2 — Install Fluent Bit on Yugabyte Nodes

Install on:

```
yb-node1
yb-node2
yb-node3
```

Fluent Bit is lightweight and designed for log shipping.

Example RHEL:

```bash
curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluent-bit/master/install.sh | sh
```

Verify:

```bash
fluent-bit --version
```

---

# Step 3 — Configure Fluent Bit Input

On each Yugabyte node:

Edit:

```bash
/etc/fluent-bit/fluent-bit.conf
```

Add:

```ini
[INPUT]
    Name tail
    Path /home/yugabyte/yb-data/tserver/logs/yb-tserver.*
    Tag yugabyte.tserver
    Parser regex
    DB /var/log/flb_yb.db
    Mem_Buf_Limit 50MB
```

This continuously tails YugabyteDB logs.

---

# Step 4 — Parse Yugabyte Log Format

Example Yugabyte log:

```text
I0715 10:32:01.123456 12345 rpc.cc:100] 
Connection established
```

Create:

```bash
/etc/fluent-bit/parsers.conf
```

Add:

```ini
[PARSER]
    Name yugabyte
    Format regex
    Regex ^(?<level>[A-Z])(?<date>[0-9]+)\s+(?<time>[0-9:]+)\s+(?<message>.*)
```

---

# Step 5 — Send Logs to Kafka

Add output:

```ini
[OUTPUT]
    Name kafka
    Match yugabyte.*
    Brokers <kafka-ip>:9092
    Topics yugabyte-logs
```

Restart:

```bash
systemctl restart fluent-bit
```

Now:

```text
Yugabyte Logs
      |
      |
 Fluent Bit
      |
      |
 Kafka
```

---

# Step 6 — Create Kafka Topic

On Kafka VM:

```bash
kafka-topics.sh \
--create \
--topic yugabyte-logs \
--bootstrap-server <kafka-ip>:9092 \
--partitions 3 \
--replication-factor 1
```

Verify:

```bash
kafka-topics.sh \
--list \
--bootstrap-server <kafka-ip>:9092
```

Output:

```
yugabyte-logs
```

---

# Step 7 — Verify Logs Enter Kafka

Run:

```bash
kafka-console-consumer.sh \
--topic yugabyte-logs \
--bootstrap-server <kafka-ip>:9092
```

You should see:

```json
{
"tag":"yugabyte.tserver",
"message":"Connection established",
"time":"10:32:01",
"level":"I"
}
```

